# LIMITS (owner 10-29 02:10 PKT: "remove any hard limits on fills, run time etc. — we can afford week-long runs"). Every cap change:
#   --minutes N           WAS 1..720 (owner 10-26: 12 h runs). NOW a whole number >= 0, no upper bound; 0 = NO TIME LIMIT (the run goes
#                         on until Ctrl-c / SIGTERM / the kill switch / the profit lock); default still 120. WHY: week-long runs.
#   --trades N            WAS required, 1..1500 (owner 10-27). NOW >= 0, no upper bound; 0 = unlimited regular live buys (engine
#                         --max-buys 0). Required only with --regular live (as before); optional (default 0) with --regular paper / off.
#                         WHY: a fixed count ends a week-long run early; the kill switch and --profit-lock guard the loss.
#   (--minutes / --trades: only a length check is left, at most 9 digits = ~1,900 years / 999,999,999 buys, because the engine reads a
#                         number it cannot parse as its DEFAULT without a word.)
#   --max-total-sol       WAS always trades x (size x 1.02 + 0.02) + 0.1. NOW the same when --trades > 0, 1000 when --trades is 0. WHY: the
#                         --dip engine never reads it (only the old --concurrent mode does), and unlimited trades have no count to multiply.
#   --presend-live-max-fills N   WAS required, 1..1000 (owner 10-27). NOW optional: absent or 0 = unlimited; if given a whole number
#                         >= 0, no upper bound; passed to the engine only when given. WHY: the owner's "no hard limits on fills".
#   --presend-live-fee-budget-sol X   WAS required, (0, 1.0] SOL and <= the dip budget left: a whole-RUN total of failed-try fees; at it
#                         the pre-send stream stopped for the rest of the run. NOW optional (engine default 1.0) and a ROLLING 24 h budget:
#                         the failed-try fees of the last 24 h; above it pre-send PAUSES until the rolling sum falls back under it (it
#                         never stops the run); if given, (0, 100] SOL. The "<= 1.0" bound and the "<= the dip budget left" check are gone
#                         (the kill switch watches the wallet balance, so failed-try fees count toward it anyway). WHY: a run total would
#                         end pre-send for good partway through a week-long run.
#   --profit-lock M[,F]   NEW (owner 10-29 02:20 PKT; it replaces the --max-drawdown-sol idea, which is dropped), off unless given: engine
#                         --profit-lock-min-sol M --profit-lock-frac F (both passed). M = SOL, (0, 100]; F = a fraction in (0, 1], default
#                         0.30. The run tracks its PEAK balance (the kill switch's balance source: balance + open positions at cost);
#                         allowed give-back G = max(M, F x (peak - start)); lock floor = peak - G; effective stop floor = max(the kill-switch
#                         floor start - LOSS (unchanged), lock floor). Below it the run stops like the kill switch: no new buys of any kind,
#                         open positions finish, the run ends (run_end reason "profit_lock"). With M below the kill switch's LOSS the lock is
#                         the tighter stop from the start (G = M while the run has no profit). WHY: the kill switch is a fixed floor (the
#                         run's start balance minus the budget left) and does not rise with a week-long run's profit.
#   UNCHANGED on purpose: the KILL SWITCH (--max-loss-sol = the dip budget left of the 0.5 SOL total across runs, from the budget file;
